{"subjectId":"sql","id":"nth-highest-salary","title":"Nth Highest Salary","group":"Interview Prep","order":55,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The most-asked SQL interview question. Know three solutions, and be precise about <strong>ties</strong> and <strong>what to return when there is no Nth value</strong>.</p>\n<p>Salaries (distinct, highest first): 150000, 120000, 110000, <strong>95000</strong> (Meera and Karan), 85000, 80000, … — so the 4th highest <em>distinct</em> salary is 95000.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-number-1\"></i>1. DENSE_RANK (best general answer)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>WITH ranked AS (\n    SELECT name, salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk\n    FROM employees\n)\nSELECT name, salary FROM ranked WHERE rnk = 4 ORDER BY name;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name  | salary\n------+-------\nKaran | 95000\nMeera | 95000</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Returns <em>every</em> employee with that salary. <code>RANK</code> would skip numbers after ties, and <code>ROW_NUMBER</code> would arbitrarily return only one of Meera/Karan and shift everyone below.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-number-2\"></i>2. LIMIT / OFFSET on distinct salaries</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT DISTINCT salary\nFROM employees\nORDER BY salary DESC\nLIMIT 1 OFFSET 3;          -- N - 1\n\n-- return NULL (not zero rows) when N is too large: wrap it in a scalar subquery\nSELECT (SELECT DISTINCT salary FROM employees ORDER BY salary DESC LIMIT 1 OFFSET 99) AS nth_salary;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>salary\n------\n95000\n\nnth_salary\n----------\nNULL</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-number-3\"></i>3. Correlated subquery (no window functions)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- the salary that has exactly N-1 distinct salaries above it\nSELECT DISTINCT e.salary\nFROM employees e\nWHERE 3 = (SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT i.salary) FROM employees i WHERE i.salary &gt; e.salary);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>salary\n------\n95000</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- 2nd highest, the classic MAX-below-MAX trick\nSELECT MAX(salary) AS second_highest\nFROM employees\nWHERE salary &lt; (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>second_highest\n--------------\n120000</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-layout-grid\"></i>Nth highest per department</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>WITH ranked AS (\n    SELECT d.name AS department, e.name, e.salary,\n           DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY e.dept_id ORDER BY e.salary DESC) AS rnk\n    FROM employees e JOIN departments d ON d.dept_id = e.dept_id\n)\nSELECT department, name, salary FROM ranked WHERE rnk = 2 ORDER BY department;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>department  | name   | salary\n------------+--------+-------\nEngineering | Ravi   | 120000\nHR          | Vikram | 80000\nSales       | Neha   | 72000</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL Server / Oracle</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT DISTINCT salary FROM employees ORDER BY salary DESC\nOFFSET 3 ROWS FETCH NEXT 1 ROWS ONLY;                  -- SQL Server 2012+ / Oracle 12c+\n\nSELECT TOP 1 salary FROM (\n    SELECT DISTINCT TOP 4 salary FROM employees ORDER BY salary DESC\n) t ORDER BY salary ASC;                               -- older SQL Server</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Which approach would you give in an interview?</summary><div><p><code>DENSE_RANK()</code> in a CTE: it handles ties correctly, generalises to “per department” with <code>PARTITION BY</code>, and reads clearly. Mention <code>LIMIT/OFFSET</code> on <code>DISTINCT</code> salaries as the short alternative, and wrap it in a scalar subquery if the spec says “return NULL if there is no Nth salary”.</p></div></details></section>"}